The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (netif_running(bp->dev)) {
    bnxt_hwrm_vnic_free_one(bp, &rss_ctx->vnic);
    for (i = 0; i < BNXT_MAX_CTX_PER_VNIC; i++) {
        if (vnic->fw_rss_cos_lb_ctx[i] != INVALID_HW_RING_ID)
            bnxt_hwrm_vnic_ctx_free_one(bp, vnic, i);
    }
}
Reason:
The `netif_running()` condition prevents freeing the VNIC firmware resources when the network interface is down. This causes a resource leak: the VNIC remains allocated in firmware, but the driver loses track of it. On a subsequent `open()`, the driver tries to restore RSS contexts and creates extra VNICs without proper reservation, leading to firmware failures and loss of active RSS contexts.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
bnxt_hwrm_vnic_free_one(bp, &rss_ctx->vnic);
for (i = 0; i < BNXT_MAX_CTX_PER_VNIC; i++) {
    if (vnic->fw_rss_cos_lb_ctx[i] != INVALID_HW_RING_ID)
        bnxt_hwrm_vnic_ctx_free_one(bp, vnic, i);
}
Reason:
Removing the `netif_running()` check ensures that VNIC and RSS context resources are always freed in firmware whenever an RSS context is deleted, regardless of the interface state. This eliminates the resource leak and prevents the subsequent failure during interface up. The firmware is designed to safely handle redundant delete requests, so the condition is unnecessary.

